== Foreword
<foreword>
Gold occupies a position in classical Indian metallurgy that is
#emph[almost] the inverse of mercury. Where mercury is liquid, reactive,
dangerous, and revered as Shiva’s seed, gold is solid, inert, safe, and
identified with the sun (Surya). Where mercury’s elaborate
eighteen-samskara purification is required to render the substance
medicinally usable, gold’s purification (#emph[shodhana]) is
comparatively simple — a sequence of quenching steps that softens and
cleans the metal without fundamentally changing its chemistry. The
contrast illuminates the tradition’s coherent logic: the more reactive
the substance, the more processing it requires; the more inert, the
less.

Gold is also the empirically most-validated metal in classical Indian
pharmacy. Modern materials science has characterized swarna-bhasma
extensively at the nanoscale. Multiple independent studies confirm that
traditional preparation methods produce #strong[face-centered cubic
crystalline gold nanoparticles in the 5-57 nm range], which is precisely
the size range pursued by contemporary gold-nanoparticle pharmaceutical
research for therapeutic applications. The ancient procedure, developed
empirically through centuries of practice, anticipates 21st-century
nanomedicine in a way no other classical preparation does.

This volume documents gold’s place in the tradition with the seriousness
it deserves and the empirical specificity the published research
enables. Where mercury required us to distinguish real chemistry from
aspirational claim, gold lets us describe convergence: classical
#emph[swarna-bhasma] is, by modern characterization, what classical
texts said it should be — #emph[bhasma] (calcined ash form) of pure
gold, finely divided, biologically incorporable.

