== 1. Identity
<identity>
=== 1.1 Substance
<substance>
Gold is the chemical element with symbol Au (from Latin #emph[aurum])
and atomic number 79. Among the noble metals, gold is the most
chemically inert under ambient conditions: it does not tarnish in air,
does not dissolve in single mineral acids, and resists most chemical
attack except aqua regia, cyanide, and a few specialized solvents. This
inertness is the foundation of both gold’s monetary role (it persists,
it doesn’t corrupt) and its medicinal claims (it can be incorporated
into the body without releasing reactive ions).

Physical properties relevant to classical metallurgy: - Density: 19.32
g/cm³ at 20°C (one of the highest among elements; the basis for the
#emph[jala-pareeksha] density test) - Melting point: 1064°C (the basis
for high-temperature #emph[puta] requirements) - Color: distinct
yellow-orange metallic luster, the eponymous "golden" color -
Malleability: extreme — pure gold can be hammered into leaf \<100 nm
thick; this property is exploited in #emph[swarna-varkha] (gold leaf)
preparation - Hardness: 2.5-3.0 Mohs (very soft for a metal) - FCC
(face-centered cubic) crystal structure, lattice constant 4.0786 Å

=== 1.2 Nomenclature
<nomenclature>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] and the #emph[Charaka Samhita], gold’s
Sanskrit names include:

- #strong[Suvarna] (सुवर्ण) — "good color"; the most common technical term
- #strong[Swarna] (स्वर्ण) — alternative spelling, common in
  Hindi-influenced contexts
- #strong[Hema] (हेम) — "lustrous"
- #strong[Hiranya] (हिरण्य) — "gleaming"; oldest Vedic term
- #strong[Kanaka] (कनक) — "shining"
- #strong[Kanchana] (कञ्चन) — "auspicious gold"
- #strong[Hataka] (हाटक) — refined gold
- #strong[Jambunada] (जाम्बूनद) — "from the Jambu river"; mythological
  reference
- #strong[Champaka] (चम्पक) — yellow like champaka flower
- #strong[Chamikara] (चामीकर) — "gold of high quality"
- #strong[Bharma] (भर्म) — "that which sustains"
- #strong[Jataroopa] (जातरूप) — "naturally formed beauty"
- #strong[Mangalyaka] (माङ्गल्यक) — "auspicious"
- #strong[Tapaniya] (तपनीय) — "that which heats" (gold conducts heat
  well)
- #strong[Agnibija] (अग्निबीज) — "fire-seed"

The terminological richness reflects gold’s cultural centrality across
Vedic, classical, and medieval Indian traditions. Gold appears in the
earliest Rigvedic hymns and never loses its symbolic primacy.

=== 1.3 Graha correspondence
<graha-correspondence>
Gold corresponds to #strong[Surya] (Sun) in the classical Indian
astrological framework. This correspondence is unanimous across primary
sources: - #emph[Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra] (BPHS) Chapter 3 -
#emph[Brihat Samhita] (Varahamihira) on planetary metallurgy -
#emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] graha-rasa identifications - #emph[Charaka
Samhita] on therapeutic correspondences

Within the dhatu/jeeva/moola classification: - #strong[Surya is
jeeva-graha] — the "living/cognitive" class (same as Budha-mercury) -
This places gold in the "conscious-substance" category alongside mercury
and Jupiter’s metals

The Surya correspondence is internally consistent with multiple
classical claims: - #strong[Color match]: gold’s yellow lustre
corresponds to solar radiance - #strong[Stability]: gold’s permanence
corresponds to Surya’s role as immutable cosmic center - #strong[Tejas]:
gold is described as embodying #emph[tejas] (radiance, vital fire), the
tattva associated with Surya in Samkhya cosmology -
#strong[Therapeutic]: gold preparations are classically indicated for
#emph[medhya] (intellect-supporting) effects, consistent with Surya’s
#emph[atma-karaka] (soul-significator) role

Some traditions (notably #emph[Brihat Samhita] in some chapters) also
assign gold a secondary association with #strong[Guru] (Jupiter) due to
gold’s classification as a "noble" metal aligned with sattvic qualities.
The Atlas framework uses Surya as primary, Guru as secondary for
multi-graha computations.

=== 1.4 Origins and Sources
<origins-and-sources>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] 5/4:

#block(inset: (left: 1em), stroke: (left: 2pt + luma(160)))[
#emph[brahmā yenā’vṛto jātaḥ suvarṇena jarāyuṇā |] \
#emph[tanmerurupatāṃ yātaṃ suvarṇaṃ sahajaṃ hi tat ||]
]

"That gold within which Brahma was wrapped at birth, which assumed the
form of Mount Meru — that is #emph[Sahaja Swarna] (natural gold)."

The text describes multiple types of gold: - #strong[Sahaja] (सहज) —
natural/innate gold, the rarest type associated with the cosmic-origin
myth - #strong[Khanija] (खनिज) — mined gold, from terrestrial sources -
#strong[Rasaja] (रसज) — gold produced through alchemical operations (the
lohavada-derived form, whose claim is not validated by modern chemistry
as discussed in Volume I §2.4.8) - #strong[Vahnija] (वह्निज) —
fire-refined gold

Historical Indian gold sources: - Kolar Gold Fields (Karnataka) —
operated from antiquity through 2001 - Hutti gold mines (Karnataka) —
still operating - Hattigudur and other South Indian deposits - Hatti,
Anantapur, Chikkaballapura placer deposits - Trade gold from Arabia, the
Levant, Africa, and (later) the Americas

The medieval Indian texts reference #emph[Suvarnadvipa] ("gold island,"
likely Sumatra) and trade-based gold acquisition, indicating that even
classical practice often used imported gold rather than purely local
material.

