== 2. The Shodhana–Marana Sequence
<the-shodhanamarana-sequence>
=== 2.1 Overview
<overview>
Gold’s purification differs structurally from mercury’s. Where mercury
required eighteen samskaras to address its reactivity, gold’s processing
uses the standard #emph[shodhana] (purification) followed by
#emph[marana] (incineration/calcination) sequence applicable to most
dhatus. The relative simplicity reflects gold’s chemical inertness: no
transformation is needed beyond mechanical refinement, surface cleaning,
and reduction to particle sizes biologically incorporable.

=== 2.2 Shodhana (Purification)
<shodhana-purification>
The classical purification protocol for gold per #emph[Rasaratna
Samuccaya] 5/9-10:

#block(inset: (left: 1em), stroke: (left: 2pt + luma(160)))[
#emph[taile takre gavāṃ mūtre hyaranāle kulatthajae |] \
#emph[kramānniṣecayettaptaṃ drāve drāve tu saptā ||]
]

"In oil, buttermilk, cow’s urine, fermented kanji, and kulattha
(horse-gram) decoction — successively, after heating, quench seven times
in each."

The sequence: 1. #strong[Taila (sesame oil)] — quenching 7 times after
heating to red-hot 2. #strong[Takra (buttermilk)] — quenching 7 times 3.
#strong[Go-mutra (cow’s urine)] — quenching 7 times 4. #strong[Aranala
(fermented kanji)] — quenching 7 times 5. #strong[Kulattha-kvatha
(horse-gram decoction)] — quenching 7 times

Total: 35 quenching cycles in 5 different media. Each cycle: heat the
gold to red-hot (~700-800°C, well below melting), then plunge into the
cool liquid medium. The gold is annealed (softened, made workable) and
surface-cleaned in each cycle.

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

The five media serve different chemistry: - #strong[Sesame oil]: organic
medium provides reducing atmosphere during quench, prevents oxidation;
surface-active sesamol/sesamin compounds clean surface films -
#strong[Buttermilk]: dilute lactic acid (pH ~4), removes oxide films
through mild acid attack; proteins coordinate trace metal contaminants -
#strong[Cow urine]: alkaline (pH ~9 fresh, becoming acidic on standing),
urea, uric acid; both removes adherent contaminants and provides
ammonium ion environment - #strong[Fermented kanji]: acidic (pH ~3.5),
strong acid attack on surface; lactic acid + acetic acid + organic acids
- #strong[Kulattha decoction]: contains amino acids and alkaloids; final
conditioning medium

The thermal cycling (heat → quench → repeat) achieves what modern
metallurgy calls #emph[thermomechanical processing]: the metal is
annealed (recrystallized) repeatedly, breaking up large crystallites and
producing a finer-grained, more workable structure. Modern gold leaf
production uses analogous repeated annealing-rolling cycles.

The published #emph[Standard Manufacturing Procedure for Swarna
Shodhana] (Khedekar et al.) found "#emph[no loss of weight observed in
gold after both purification]" — confirming what the chemistry would
predict. Gold is inert; quenching cycles clean the surface and refine
the microstructure but don’t remove gold itself.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Standardized procedures exist for both
#emph[samanya] (general) and #emph[vishesha] (specific) shodhana of
gold.

=== 2.3 Marana (Incineration)
<marana-incineration>
The transformation from purified gold to #emph[swarna-bhasma] requires
repeated #emph[puta] (calcination) cycles with specific co-substances.
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] and standardized in modern practice:

#strong[Procedure:] 1. #emph[Shuddha Swarna] (purified gold) is rolled
into thin foils (#emph[swarna-varkha], traditionally \<100 nm thick) 2.
Equal parts purified mercury (#emph[shuddha parada]) and purified sulfur
(#emph[shuddha gandhaka]) are triturated together to form #emph[kajjali]
(black HgS-mercury mixture, see Volume I §4.2) 3. Gold foils are
sandwiched within kajjali; the mixture is triturated with
#emph[nimbu-swarasa] (lemon juice) or #emph[kumari-swarasa] (aloe juice)
4. Cakes (#emph[chakrika]) are formed and dried in shade or sun 5. The
dried cakes are placed inside #emph[sharava-samputa] (two earthen plates
joined and sealed) and subjected to #emph[gajaputa] (large fire pit)
heating 6. The cycle is repeated 14-30 times until traditional tests
confirm completion

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

This is #emph[mechano-chemical synthesis of gold nanoparticles]. The
mechanism: - Gold foil (bulk metal) is reduced to thin form via
mechanical processing - Kajjali (mercury-sulfur mixture) provides
amalgamation of gold (Au-Hg amalgam forms readily at room temperature) -
Lemon juice (citric acid pH ~2.4) provides acidic medium and
citrate-like complexing - Heating to ~600°C in sealed crucible drives
off mercury (Hg vaporizes ~357°C boiling) and sulfur (sublimes ~115°C,
decomposes various S compounds) - What remains is finely-divided gold;
subsequent re-amalgamation and re-heating progressively reduces particle
size - After 14-30 cycles, particles reach nanoscale (5-57 nm reported)

The lemon juice + heat treatment effectively mirrors the #emph[citrate
reduction synthesis] used in modern gold nanoparticle production
(Turkevich method, 1951): gold ions are reduced to Au⁰ in the presence
of citric acid, producing colloidal gold nanoparticles. The classical
procedure is essentially a more elaborate, multi-cycle version of the
same chemistry.

#strong[Modern characterization (peer-reviewed):]

Multiple independent studies have characterized swarna-bhasma:

#align(center)[#table(
  columns: 3,
  align: (col, row) => (auto,auto,auto,).at(col),
  inset: 6pt,
  [Reference], [Method], [Findings],
  [Singh et al. 2021 (J Ayurveda Integr Med)],
  [XRD, FE-SEM, FE-TEM, ICP-AES, EDXRF],
  [FCC nano-gold, 5-20 nm spherical particles, 95% Au gravimetric],
  [Bhowmick et al. 2009 (Pharmacog Rev)],
  [XRD, SEM, EDAX, AAS, FT-IR],
  [Crystalline, \>98% gold, particles \<9 μm with nanoscale features],
  [Khedekar et al. 2015 (Annals Ayurvedic Med)],
