== 3. Major Gold Preparations
<major-gold-preparations>
=== 3.1 Swarna-Bhasma (Gold Calx)
<swarna-bhasma-gold-calx>
#strong[Composition:] ≥95% Au, FCC nanocrystalline, particle size 5-57
nm depending on preparation method \
#strong[Color:] light brown to dark brown (counterintuitive given gold’s
gold color — at nanoscale, gold absorbs in different wavelengths and
appears non-yellow) \
#strong[Source text:] Rasaratna Samuccaya, Rasendra Chudamani, Rasa
Tarangini, Bhaishajya Ratnavali

#strong[Therapeutic indications (classical):] - #emph[Medhya]
(intellect-supporting) - #emph[Rasayana] (rejuvenating) -
#emph[Vajikarana] (vitalizing) - #emph[Hridya] (cardiotonic) - Indicated
in: #emph[jirnajwara] (chronic fever), #emph[kshaya] (wasting),
#emph[unmada] (mental disturbance), #emph[medhakshaya] (cognitive
decline), #emph[vatavyadhi] (neurological disorders)

#strong[Modern pharmacology research:] - Anti-inflammatory effects
documented in arthritis models (gold compounds have been used in Western
medicine for rheumatoid arthritis since 1929) - Antimicrobial activity
confirmed against gut pathobionts (Sharma et al. 2024, #emph[Biological
Trace Element Research]) - Cognitive enhancement effects under
investigation - Gold nanoparticles in size range 20-50 nm have
demonstrated anti-arthritic effects in rat models (cited in multiple
swarna-bhasma reviews)

#strong[Dosage:] 15-125 mg twice daily classical; modern licensed
Ayurvedic practice typically 30-60 mg daily under physician supervision

#strong[Toxicity:] Gold is biologically inert in elemental form;
concerns relate primarily to traditional preparation methods that may
use mercury (residual mercury contamination), and to preparations that
have not undergone proper marana (residual reactive gold ions). Properly
prepared swarna-bhasma per modern characterization has #emph[absolute
absence of mercury] (Singh et al. 2021 ICP-AES findings).

=== 3.2 Swarna-Sindura (Gold-Vermilion compound)
<swarna-sindura-gold-vermilion-compound>
#strong[Composition:] Au-Hg-S compound, predominantly HgS with embedded
gold nanoparticles \
#strong[Note:] This is essentially Makaradhwaja (covered in Volume I
§4.4) — a kupipakwa preparation containing all three of swarna, parada,
gandhaka

#strong[Cross-reference]: See Volume I §4.4 for full treatment.
Classified as gold-preparation in some contexts (because gold is the
"active" ingredient) and as mercury-preparation in others (because
mercury is the bulk substance).

=== 3.3 Swarnaprash (Gold-honey-ghee preparation)
<swarnaprash-gold-honey-ghee-preparation>
#strong[Composition:] Swarna-bhasma + honey (madhu) + cow ghee (ghrita)
± other rasayana herbs \
#strong[Particle size in final preparation:] 0.6-9.5 μm (Sharma et
al. 2024) \
#strong[Use:] Classical pre-lacteal feed for neonates (administered in
drops to newborns); modern continued use as immunostimulant for children

#strong[Modern characterization:] - Gold particles in Swarnaprash are
larger than in pure swarna-bhasma (because the bhasma is dispersed in
viscous honey-ghee matrix and aggregates can re-form) - Antimicrobial
activity confirmed against neonatal gut pathobionts while supporting
beneficial symbiont strains - Mechanism likely involves both direct
antimicrobial action of gold nanoparticles and immunomodulatory effects

#strong[Important note]: Despite extensive traditional use, modern
medical guidance is cautious about administration of any gold-based
preparation to neonates. Quality control of commercial swarnaprash
varies enormously; only AYUSH-licensed preparations should be used, and
only under qualified pediatric Ayurvedic supervision.

=== 3.4 Swarnamakshika-Bhasma (different substance, included for
clarity)
<swarnamakshika-bhasma-different-substance-included-for-clarity>
Despite the similar name, #emph[swarnamakshika] is #strong[not a gold
preparation]. It is #strong[chalcopyrite (CuFeS₂)], a copper-iron
sulfide mineral. The name reflects its golden surface luster, not its
composition.

After processing (shodhana with triphala kvatha, marana through 9+
putas), the resulting bhasma contains: - Fe₂O₃ (hematite) - FeS₂
(pyrite, residual) - CuS (covellite) - SiO₂

Treated thoroughly in dedicated Volume on #emph[uparasas] (secondary
minerals). Mentioned here only to disambiguate from actual gold
preparations.

=== 3.5 Suvarna-Vanga and other gold alloys
<suvarna-vanga-and-other-gold-alloys>
Classical metallurgy includes gold alloys with specific
cosmological-medical correspondences: - #strong[Swarna-tara] (gold +
silver) — Surya + Chandra integration - #strong[Swarna-tamra] (gold +
copper) — Surya + Mangala (sometimes prepared as "rose gold" alloys) -
#strong[Swarna-vanga] (gold + tin) — Surya + Guru integration

These alloys appear in ashtadhatu (see Volume I cross-reference and the
panchaloha analysis paper); they are not separate single-metal
preparations but are listed here to indicate gold’s role in alloy
contexts.

