== 6. Empirical Research Targets
<empirical-research-targets>
=== 6.1 Highest-leverage tests (specific to gold)
<highest-leverage-tests-specific-to-gold>
#strong[Test 1: Particle-size variance across timing windows] -
Hypothesis: framework-favorable timing produces tighter particle-size
distribution in swarna-bhasma - Method: prepare batches on
Krittika/Uttara-Phalguni/Uttara-Ashadha (Surya-friendly) vs
Pushya/Anuradha (Surya-unfriendly per framework); characterize particle
size distribution by DLS - Equipment: DLS instrument (~\$30-60K,
available at any university materials lab) - Statistical power: 5+
batches per condition needed - Estimated study cost: \$50-100K including
labor for 1 year

#strong[Test 2: Crystallographic perfection variance] - Hypothesis:
framework-favorable timing produces narrower XRD peak FWHM (better
crystallinity) - Method: same batches, characterize by XRD; calculate
Scherrer crystallite size and peak FWHM - Equipment: XRD instrument
(available at any university) - Estimated study cost: \$30-50K

#strong[Test 3: Therapeutic efficacy variance] - Hypothesis:
framework-favorable timing produces bhasma with measurably different
bioactivity - Method: arthritis or cognition models in rats; randomized
batch source - Caveat: confounded by particle-size effects (which would
be primary mechanism) - Estimated cost: \$200K+; requires animal
facility and long-term commitment

=== 6.2 Realistic constraints
<realistic-constraints>
- #strong[Material costs]: gold is expensive; multiple batches ×
  multiple conditions \= significant material budget
- #strong[Time costs]: each puta cycle is 24-48 hours; 30-cycle marana
  takes weeks
- #strong[Practitioner availability]: skilled vaidya needed; their
  schedule constraints affect what timing windows can actually be tested
- #strong[Statistical power]: nakshatra changes every ~14 hours, so
  timing-window experiments have natural granularity issues

=== 6.3 Lower-cost preliminary tests
<lower-cost-preliminary-tests>
Before full Test 1: a #emph[retrospective] study using existing
licensed-pharmacy swarna-bhasma batches: - Multiple batches from
quality-controlled producers exist - Production dates may be available
in QC records - Cross-reference dates with framework predictions - If
existing data shows trend, full prospective study is justified - Cost:
minimal — just data analysis if pharmacies will share QC records

This retrospective approach has the same logic that allowed the
panchaloha structural validation: use existing data rather than generate
new data, work with what the field already has.

